[
    {
        "content": "<p>Given a subgroup H of a group G, there are two ways to say the underlying set of H:<br>\n<code>H.carrier</code> and <code>(H : Set G)</code>. <br>\nThey are definitionally equal. However, in pratice they behave differently in proofs. For example,</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">lemma</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">stabilizer_conj_coe</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Representation</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">stabilizer</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">carrier</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">op</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"bp\">⁻¹</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">stabilizer</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">carrier</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">ext</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">only</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">stabilizer</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">mem_setOf_eq</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>works, while</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">lemma</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">stabilizer_conj_coe</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Representation</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">stabilizer</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"n\">op</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"bp\">⁻¹</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">stabilizer</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">ext</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">only</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">stabilizer</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">mem_setOf_eq</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>unfolds stabilizer into several Subtypes so that <code>mem_setOf_eq</code> does not apply. In some other examples (H : Set G) template works better. Could someone explain to me what is the difference on earth and which one is preferred?</p>",
        "id": 605991160,
        "sender_full_name": "Jiaxi Mo",
        "timestamp": 1782218418
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Use <code>(H : Set G)</code> (and try to avoid breaking apart definitions like <code>stabilizer</code> if you can help it)</p>",
        "id": 605992027,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1782218597
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(If you provide an <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mwe.html\">#mwe</a> then it will be easier to suggest alternative approaches)</p>",
        "id": 605992385,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1782218688
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Can you please write a <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mwe.html\">#mwe</a>?</p>",
        "id": 605992483,
        "sender_full_name": "Riccardo Brasca",
        "timestamp": 1782218715
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Anyway in my opinion this is just because of <code>simp only</code> taking different route (using the general <code>SetLike</code> instance or not). I agree that you should just use <code>(H : Set G)</code>, and in general <em>don't unfold</em> definitions.</p>",
        "id": 605993343,
        "sender_full_name": "Riccardo Brasca",
        "timestamp": 1782218949
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ideally, there is some <code>mem_stabilizer_iff</code> lemma which you can provide to simp...</p>",
        "id": 605996874,
        "sender_full_name": "Edward van de Meent",
        "timestamp": 1782219773
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Actually nvm, the right thing is that the <code>.carrier</code> projection should simp away to the coercion</p>",
        "id": 605997346,
        "sender_full_name": "Edward van de Meent",
        "timestamp": 1782219895
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You can review it (about 100 lines) here: <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/40941\">#40941</a>. The example above is the last lemma. I did write a lemma <code>mem_stabilizer</code>. However, <code>mem_stabilizer</code> does not work well in the last lemma, so I unfold <code>stabilizer</code> anyway (and works exatly way I hope for <code>(H : Set G)</code>). (Unfortunately, I used <code>.carrier</code> <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"130384\">@Riccardo Brasca</span> <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"253861\">@Thomas Browning</span> .....)</p>",
        "id": 605999135,
        "sender_full_name": "Jiaxi Mo",
        "timestamp": 1782220325
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If you add <code>stabilizer</code> and you have to unfold it it means you are missing some API. In general the definition should be used only in ridiculously trivial lemmas, you should never see <code>simp only [stabilizer]</code> again.</p>",
        "id": 606003149,
        "sender_full_name": "Riccardo Brasca",
        "timestamp": 1782221170
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"130384\">@Riccardo Brasca</span> Thanks for pointing this out! I think I detected an issue, but confusing to me. Here below is the one I used in mem_stabilizer</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">lemma</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">mem_stabilizer</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Representation</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">stabilizer</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>mem_stabilizer</code> fails to <code>simp</code> when the goal of the form <code>x ∈ (ρ.stabilizer (ρ g v)).carrier</code>. If I add another stupid lemma, namely</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">lemma</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">mem_stabilizer_coe</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Representation</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"n\">stabilizer</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">G</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Then it applies directly. Should I include <code>mem_stabilizer_coe</code>?</p>",
        "id": 606027290,
        "sender_full_name": "Jiaxi Mo",
        "timestamp": 1782226822
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>No, you should first rewrite with <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=SetLike.mem_coe#doc\">docs#SetLike.mem_coe</a> (or something like that)</p>",
        "id": 606027806,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1782226937
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks! The relevant theorem is<code>Subgroup.mem_carrier</code>. There is no @[simp] above this stupid theorem <code>Subgroup.mem_carrier</code>. Should we add a @[simp]? Puzzle solved!</p>",
        "id": 606031558,
        "sender_full_name": "Jiaxi Mo",
        "timestamp": 1782227816
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think use of <code>.carrier</code> is discouraged, so there's not much need?</p>",
        "id": 606032012,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1782227940
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I see. I will avoid it in the future. Thanks a lot! </p>\n<p>If I use the <code>(H : Set G)</code> version, <code>simp [mem_stabilizer]</code> works well. It is indeed the <code>.carrier</code> discouraged issue.</p>",
        "id": 606037879,
        "sender_full_name": "Jiaxi Mo",
        "timestamp": 1782229484
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think this is the way to go!</p>",
        "id": 606042781,
        "sender_full_name": "Riccardo Brasca",
        "timestamp": 1782230766
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"1097608\">Jiaxi Mo</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113488-general/topic/Differences.20between.20H.2Ecarrier.20and.20.28H.20.3A.20Set.20G.29/near/606031558\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Thanks! The relevant theorem is<code>Subgroup.mem_carrier</code>. There is no @[simp] above this stupid theorem <code>Subgroup.mem_carrier</code>. Should we add a @[simp]? Puzzle solved!</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Similar lemmas are simp in mathlib to make discoverable the fact that this spelling is discouraged. Please add the simp lemma!</p>",
        "id": 606158389,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1782281090
    }
]